    "gold_confidence": "medium",
    "gold_justification": "The primary outcome was null (no effect on acute infection severity). The secondary\nfinding (4 percentage points) is small and framed with 'promising signal' — optimistic\nlanguage that exceeds what a null primary + weak secondary warrants. The hedge 'larger\ntrials are needed' accompanies but does not constrain the optimism. This pattern of\ndecorative uncertainty + small effect + null primary = mild overclaim.\n",
    "gold_alternative_reading": "A 'solid' reading is defensible: the senior author explicitly calls for larger trials,\nthe effect size is not exaggerated (reported accurately as 21% vs 25%), and 'promising\nsignal' could be seen as appropriately tentative language for a secondary finding. All\nthree Claude models (Haiku, Sonnet, Opus) consistently classify this as 'solid' across\ntwo validation sprints (0/6 correct). This systematic disagreement suggests the case\nsits on the overclaim/solid boundary.\n",
    "gold_case_type": "borderline",
    "curator_notes": "Central calibration case. 0/6 model runs across two sprints agree with the gold label.\nAll models see 'promising signal' + 'larger trials needed' as responsible science\ncommunication. Consider whether this case is better classified as borderline-solid or\nwhether the gold standard reflects a stricter epistemic norm than current LLMs apply.\nRetain as overclaim for now to test whether future model improvements or prompt changes\ncan detect the decorative uncertainty pattern. The hedge-overclaim detector correctly\nflags the suspicious pattern in Sprint 2 (Opus).\n"
  },
